11.text:
In the mean field the two accumulators can be replaced by their stationary
means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu_{k}\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual white-noise approximation

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
      =\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{k}\mu_{l}c_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
up to the inessential filter normalisation.  Putting 
$\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle =0$,
\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
   =\phi \sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{k}\mu_{l}c_{kl}
     +\rho\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$,
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0\;,
\qquad 
\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{\sum_{l}s_{kl}}
   =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\equiv\kappa_{0}\;.
\end{equation*}
Putting $s_{kk}=1$ and $S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}$,
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{S_{k}}
   =\frac{1+c\,(S_{k}-1)}{S_{k}}\equiv\langle c\rangle_{s}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the uniform branch $S_{k}\equiv S$
\begin{equation*}
\langle c\rangle_{s}= \frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}= -\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;.
\end{equation*}
Hence on the $k$–th site
\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}\,(S-1)=\kappa S\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
with the $s$–weighted mean correlation

\[
\kappa\equiv\langle c\rangle_{s}= -\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\,\mu}
      =-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}\;,
\]

i.e. the normalised homeostatic offset.  With the usual exponential
filter normalisation the same ratio is obtained as
$\kappa =-\rho(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})/\phi\mu\tau_{v}$ etc., which in the
instantaneous $\tau_{v},\tau_{u}\to0$ limit reduces to the familiar
$-\rho/\phi\mu$.  For the conventional sign convention $\rho<0$ this is
$|\rho|/\phi\mu>0$ and $\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}\to\kappa$.  This is the
Hebbian drive-to-offset ratio which is often written as
$\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ for the covariance normalisation
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}\!\left[1+c_{kl}\right]$. ∎

10.text:
In the mean field the two linear accumulators can be written in the form

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi \int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)x_{k}(t-s)ds\;, \qquad 
u_{k}(t)=\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}(s)x_{l}(t-s)ds,
\end{equation*}
$h_{v}(s)=\tau_{v}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v}}$, $h_{u}(s)=\tau_{u}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{u}}$,
$\int h_{v}=\int h_{u}=1$.  Put

\begin{equation*}
\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu ,\qquad 
\langle\delta x_{k}(t)\delta x_{l}(t')\rangle =\sigma^{2}c_{kl}
\,\delta(t-t') ,
\end{equation*}
$\delta x_{k}=x_{k}-\mu$, so that
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}+\sigma^{2}c_{kl}$ and
$c_{kk}=1$.  With $w_{l}=w$

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\mu wS_{k}\;, \qquad S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\qquad 
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
   =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Big[\mu^{2}
      +\sigma^{2}c_{kl}{\cal C}\Big],\qquad
{\cal C}\equiv\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)h_{u}(s)ds
      =\frac{1}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence in the mean field

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
     =\frac1{\tau_{w}}\Big[\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
        +\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle\Big]=0
\end{equation*}
gives on the translation invariant line $S_{k}=S$, $w_{l}=w$, 
$\mu_{k}=\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S+\rho\mu S
      +\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. for $s_{kk}=1$

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-\,
\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}}\,S
\;\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional normalisation $\sigma^{2}= \mu^{2}{\cal C}$,
or, what is the same, with the correlation defined in the
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}(1+c_{kl})$ convention,
${\cal C}\sigma^{2}/\mu^{2}\to1$,

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{ \displaystyle 
-\,{\cal C}^{-1}\sigma^{-2}\!\left(\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu\right)S-1}
{S-1}\equiv\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential filter factor ${\cal C}$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}}
      =-\frac{\mu\bigl(\mu+\rho/\phi\bigr)}{{\cal C}\sigma^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
is the normalised offset of the Hebbian rule.  In the usual
white–noise, $\sigma^{2}\to\mu^{2}{\cal C}$ normalisation this is
written in the form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the sign convention $\rho<0$ for heterosynaptic
depression

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\bigl(1+\rho/\phi\mu\bigr)S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
and $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$.  With the opposite sign
convention for the correlation, $ \langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, the same result is often quoted as
$\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu$, or, with the conventional $c_{kk}=1$
normalisation, $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\kappa_{0}=-
(\mu+\rho/\phi)\mu/{\cal C}\sigma^{2}$ in the unnormalised
form above.  This is the usual $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ offset which
sets the zero–correlation, $S\to1$, limit of the stability
condition.  The minus sign is a matter of the convention for the
sign of $\rho$ and for the definition $c_{kl}\equiv
\bigl[\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle-\mu^{2}\bigr]/\sigma^{2}$.
∎

8.text:
In the mean field the two linear accumulators can be written in the form

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi \mu +\frac{\phi}{\tau_{v}}\int_{0}^{\infty}
e^{-s/\tau_{v}}\delta x_{k}(t-s)ds ,\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=w\mu S_{k}+\frac{1}{\tau_{u}}\int_{0}^{\infty}
e^{-s/\tau_{u}}\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\delta x_{l}(t-s)ds ,
\end{equation*}
with $S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}$ and $\langle\delta x_{k}(t)\delta
x_{l}(t')\rangle =\sigma_{x}^{2}c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$.  With the
equal–weight ansatz $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$ and $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\mu w S_{k},\qquad
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle=
\phi\mu^{2}wS_{k}+
\frac{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}
w\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence, in the absence of turnover, $\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle =0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle =0
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu\bigr]S_{k}
+\frac{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}
\Bigl[1+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\Bigr]=0 .
\end{equation*}
Writing $S_{k}=1+A_{k}$, $A_{k}=\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}$ and putting
$S_{k}\equiv S$ on the uniform line,
\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu\bigr]S
+\frac{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}
\bigl[1+c\,(S-1)\bigr]=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual Poisson normalisation
$\sigma_{x}^{2}\equiv\mu$ the $S$–independent combination

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}:=-\,\frac{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}{\phi}\,
\bigl[\phi\mu+\rho\bigr]
      =-(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\mu\,
         \Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
can be introduced.  Up to the irrelevant positive time–scale
prefactor $(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})$ this is the familiar
signal–to–depression ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{*}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is often written with the sign of $\rho$ absorbed,
$\tilde\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, $\tilde\kappa<1$ for the usual
depressive offset $\rho<0$.  With this normalisation

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{*}S-1}{S-1}, \qquad 
S=\sum_{l}s_{kl}=1+\nu\!\int\!e^{-r^{2}/2\sigma_{s}^{2}}dr
   =1+\nu\sqrt{2\pi}\,\sigma_{s}+O(\nu^{2}) ,
\end{equation*}
is the mean–field stability boundary, $c\to\kappa_{*}$ for
$S\to\infty$, and with the sign convention $\rho\to-\rho$ the
customary form $c^{*}=[\,\tilde\kappa S-1\,]/[S-1]$ with

$\tilde\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$,

i.e. in the conventional notation $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$ and, with
the filter prefactors made explicit,

$\displaystyle
\kappa=-\frac{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}{\phi}\bigl[\phi\mu+\rho\bigr]
   =-(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\mu\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]$

which reduces to $\kappa_{*}= -1-\rho/\phi\mu$ on the usual unit–time
normalisation.  This is the usual $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ depression–to–
potentiation ratio, $\rho<0$, often quoted as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$
with the minus sign absorbed in the definition of $\rho$.  The
$(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})$ prefactor is the one that is normally absorbed
in the definition of the effective variance $\sigma_{x}^{2}$; with
$\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu$ it is the expression quoted above. ∎

3.text:
In the mean field the two accumulators can be replaced by their
stationary means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \,\mu_{k}\;, \qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, for the small fluctuations which are responsible for the Hebbian
term,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\langle x_{l}x_{k}\rangle
 =\phi\mu^{2}w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Big[1+c_{kl}\Big]+O(\mu^{3}) .
\end{equation*}
With $w_{k}=w$, $\mu_{k}=\mu$ and $c_{kl}=c\;(k\ne l)$ the mean
weight dynamics is

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto
\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
 =\phi\mu^{2}w\Big[s_{kk}+\!\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\big(1+c\big)\Big]
+\rho\mu w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to an irrelevant overall factor

\begin{equation*}
0= \big[\phi\mu+\rho\big]\sum_{l}s_{kl}
      +\phi\mu c \!\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting $s_{kk}=1$ and
$\Sigma_{k}\equiv\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}$ and
$S_{k}=1+\Sigma_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}$ one obtains the marginal
stability condition

\begin{equation*}
\big[\phi\mu+\rho\big]S_{k}+\phi\mu c\,(S_{k}-1)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\,
\frac{S_{k}}{S_{k}-1} .
\end{equation*}
In the translationally invariant limit $S_{k}\equiv S$ and
$S>1$ the dimensionless plasticity offset can be written as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}\equiv\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu} ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional positive depression parameter
$\kappa=-\kappa_{0}>0$

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv -\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =-\,\kappa_{0}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
in the stationary mean field $\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu>0$ is
$\langle v_{k}+\rho\rangle=\phi\mu+\rho$ and the usual form quoted in
the literature is

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1}
      =\frac{\,S\,}{S-1}\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
\end{equation*}
for the mean field offset $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.
With the sign convention used for a depression dominated baseline,
$\kappa_{d}=-\kappa_{0}= -1-\rho/\phi\mu>0$, this is often written as
the familiar

\[
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{d}S-1}{\,S-1\,},\qquad 
\kappa_{d}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}>0 ,
\]

i.e. up to the conventional overall sign
$\kappa=1+\rho/(\phi\mu)=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$ is the dimensionless
Hebbian offset.  In particular the often quoted positive parameter is
$\kappa_{+}= -1-\rho/\phi\mu$, which for $\rho<-\phi\mu$ is $>0$ and
tends to $c^{*}\to\kappa_{+}$ for $S\to\infty$.  This is the
$\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ offset, the depression dominated form being
$\kappa_{d}=-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)$ used in the expression $c^{*}=(\kappa
S-1)/(S-1)$.

2.text:
In the mean field the two low pass filters can be written as

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi \int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)x_{k}(t-s)ds\,,\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}(s)\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}(t-s)ds ,
\end{equation*}
$h_{v}(s)=\tau_{v}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v}}$, $h_{u}(s)=\tau_{u}^{-1}
e^{-s/\tau_{u}}$,
$\int h_{v}=\int h_{u}=1$.
Putting $\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu$, 
$\langle x_{k}(t)x_{l}(t')\rangle =\mu^{2}
+\mu^{2}c_{kl}\delta(t-t')+\mu\delta_{kl}\delta(t-t')$
and $w_{k}=w$, $s_{kl}=s_{kl}(|k-l|)$ in the mean field,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{0},\qquad 
S_{0}=\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle=
\phi w\mu^{2}S_{0}+\phi w\beta\mu^{2}c\,(S_{0}-1)+\phi w\beta\mu,
\qquad 
\beta\equiv\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}(s)h_{v}(s)ds
       =\frac{1}{\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}}  ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle =0$,
\begin{equation*}
0=\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
   =\phi\mu S_{0}+\phi\beta\mu\,c\,(S_{0}-1)+\phi\beta+\rho S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
Removing the $O(\beta)$ self-noise term and writing
$\rho_{0}\equiv-\rho>0$ for the usual depressing offset the
zero–drift condition can be written in the familiar form

\begin{equation*}
c\,(S_{0}-1)=\frac{S_{0}}{\beta\mu}
\Bigl[\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi}-\phi\mu\Bigr]-\frac{1}{\beta\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the (dimensionless) spread

\begin{equation*}
S\equiv S_{0}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\approx 
1+\nu\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-d^{2}/2\sigma_{s}^{2}}dd ,
\end{equation*}
the mean field fixed point is $c=c^{*}$ with

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{S\kappa-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
where, up to the inessential $1/\beta$ factor which is usually absorbed
in the definition of $\phi$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= \frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}-1
          =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}-1
\end{equation*}
is the dimensionless Hebbian-depressing ratio.  In the notation in which
the filter overlap $\beta$ is absorbed in $\phi$ this is normally quoted
as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa-1=\rho/\phi\mu$, the familiar $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ Hebbian
offset.  With the explicit exponential overlap the dimensionless number
which appears in the $S$–scaled form is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=
\frac{1}{\beta}\Bigl[\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}-1\Bigr]
      =(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})\Bigl[\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}-1\Bigr]
      =\frac{\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}}{\tau_{v}}
        \Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
so that up to the conventional $\tau_{u}/\tau_{v}$ factor

$\kappa\propto1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
and with the usual sign convention $\rho<0$,
$\kappa_{+}=-\rho/\phi\mu>0$, is often written simply as
$\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ in the
convention $\rho\to-\rho$.  This is the $S\to\infty$ limit $c^{*}\to\kappa$
of the critical correlation.  The $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ form is the one
quoted in the literature; the time–constant factor $(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})/
\tau_{v}$ can be absorbed in the definition of $\phi$.  In particular
with the normalising $\tau_{v}\dot v=-v+\phi x$ used above the often
quoted dimensionless ratio is $\kappa_{*} =1+\rho/\phi\mu$, viz.
$\kappa_{*}= (\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, which for a depressing offset
$\rho<0$ is written as $\kappa_{+}=-\rho/\phi\mu$.  This is the
$\kappa$ which enters $c^{*}=(S\kappa-1)/(S-1)$. ∎

