sets the zero–correlation, $S\to1$, limit of the stability
condition.  The minus sign is a matter of the convention for the
sign of $\rho$ and for the definition $c_{kl}\equiv
\bigl[\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle-\mu^{2}\bigr]/\sigma^{2}$.
∎

public_text.8:
In the mean field the two linear accumulators can be written in the form

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi \mu +\frac{\phi}{\tau_{v}}\int_{0}^{\infty}
e^{-s/\tau_{v}}\delta x_{k}(t-s)ds ,\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=w\mu S_{k}+\frac{1}{\tau_{u}}\int_{0}^{\infty}
e^{-s/\tau_{u}}\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\delta x_{l}(t-s)ds ,
\end{equation*}
with $S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}$ and $\langle\delta x_{k}(t)\delta
x_{l}(t')\rangle =\sigma_{x}^{2}c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$.  With the
equal–weight ansatz $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$ and $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\mu w S_{k},\qquad
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle=
\phi\mu^{2}wS_{k}+
\frac{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}
w\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence, in the absence of turnover, $\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle =0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle =0
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu\bigr]S_{k}
+\frac{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}
\Bigl[1+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\Bigr]=0 .
\end{equation*}
Writing $S_{k}=1+A_{k}$, $A_{k}=\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}$ and putting
$S_{k}\equiv S$ on the uniform line,
\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu\bigr]S
+\frac{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}
\bigl[1+c\,(S-1)\bigr]=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual Poisson normalisation
$\sigma_{x}^{2}\equiv\mu$ the $S$–independent combination

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}:=-\,\frac{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}{\phi}\,
\bigl[\phi\mu+\rho\bigr]
      =-(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\mu\,
         \Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
can be introduced.  Up to the irrelevant positive time–scale
prefactor $(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})$ this is the familiar
signal–to–depression ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{*}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is often written with the sign of $\rho$ absorbed,
$\tilde\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, $\tilde\kappa<1$ for the usual
depressive offset $\rho<0$.  With this normalisation

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{*}S-1}{S-1}, \qquad 
S=\sum_{l}s_{kl}=1+\nu\!\int\!e^{-r^{2}/2\sigma_{s}^{2}}dr
   =1+\nu\sqrt{2\pi}\,\sigma_{s}+O(\nu^{2}) ,
\end{equation*}
is the mean–field stability boundary, $c\to\kappa_{*}$ for
$S\to\infty$, and with the sign convention $\rho\to-\rho$ the
customary form $c^{*}=[\,\tilde\kappa S-1\,]/[S-1]$ with

$\tilde\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$,

i.e. in the conventional notation $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$ and, with
the filter prefactors made explicit,

$\displaystyle
\kappa=-\frac{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}{\phi}\bigl[\phi\mu+\rho\bigr]
   =-(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\mu\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]$

which reduces to $\kappa_{*}= -1-\rho/\phi\mu$ on the usual unit–time
normalisation.  This is the usual $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ depression–to–
potentiation ratio, $\rho<0$, often quoted as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$
with the minus sign absorbed in the definition of $\rho$.  The
$(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})$ prefactor is the one that is normally absorbed
in the definition of the effective variance $\sigma_{x}^{2}$; with
$\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu$ it is the expression quoted above. ∎

public_text.3:
In the mean field the two accumulators can be replaced by their
stationary means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \,\mu_{k}\;, \qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, for the small fluctuations which are responsible for the Hebbian
term,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\langle x_{l}x_{k}\rangle
 =\phi\mu^{2}w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Big[1+c_{kl}\Big]+O(\mu^{3}) .
\end{equation*}
With $w_{k}=w$, $\mu_{k}=\mu$ and $c_{kl}=c\;(k\ne l)$ the mean
weight dynamics is

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto
\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
 =\phi\mu^{2}w\Big[s_{kk}+\!\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\big(1+c\big)\Big]
+\rho\mu w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to an irrelevant overall factor

\begin{equation*}
0= \big[\phi\mu+\rho\big]\sum_{l}s_{kl}
      +\phi\mu c \!\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting $s_{kk}=1$ and
$\Sigma_{k}\equiv\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}$ and
$S_{k}=1+\Sigma_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}$ one obtains the marginal
stability condition

\begin{equation*}
\big[\phi\mu+\rho\big]S_{k}+\phi\mu c\,(S_{k}-1)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\,
\frac{S_{k}}{S_{k}-1} .
\end{equation*}
In the translationally invariant limit $S_{k}\equiv S$ and
$S>1$ the dimensionless plasticity offset can be written as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}\equiv\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu} ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional positive depression parameter
$\kappa=-\kappa_{0}>0$

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv -\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =-\,\kappa_{0}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
in the stationary mean field $\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu>0$ is
$\langle v_{k}+\rho\rangle=\phi\mu+\rho$ and the usual form quoted in
the literature is

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1}
      =\frac{\,S\,}{S-1}\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
\end{equation*}
for the mean field offset $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.
With the sign convention used for a depression dominated baseline,
$\kappa_{d}=-\kappa_{0}= -1-\rho/\phi\mu>0$, this is often written as
the familiar

\[
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{d}S-1}{\,S-1\,},\qquad 
\kappa_{d}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}>0 ,
\]

i.e. up to the conventional overall sign
$\kappa=1+\rho/(\phi\mu)=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$ is the dimensionless
Hebbian offset.  In particular the often quoted positive parameter is
$\kappa_{+}= -1-\rho/\phi\mu$, which for $\rho<-\phi\mu$ is $>0$ and
tends to $c^{*}\to\kappa_{+}$ for $S\to\infty$.  This is the
$\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ offset, the depression dominated form being
$\kappa_{d}=-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)$ used in the expression $c^{*}=(\kappa
S-1)/(S-1)$.

public_text.2:
In the mean field the two low pass filters can be written as

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi \int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)x_{k}(t-s)ds\,,\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}(s)\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}(t-s)ds ,
\end{equation*}
$h_{v}(s)=\tau_{v}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v}}$, $h_{u}(s)=\tau_{u}^{-1}
e^{-s/\tau_{u}}$,
$\int h_{v}=\int h_{u}=1$.
Putting $\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu$, 
$\langle x_{k}(t)x_{l}(t')\rangle =\mu^{2}
+\mu^{2}c_{kl}\delta(t-t')+\mu\delta_{kl}\delta(t-t')$
and $w_{k}=w$, $s_{kl}=s_{kl}(|k-l|)$ in the mean field,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{0},\qquad 
S_{0}=\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle=
\phi w\mu^{2}S_{0}+\phi w\beta\mu^{2}c\,(S_{0}-1)+\phi w\beta\mu,
\qquad 
\beta\equiv\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}(s)h_{v}(s)ds
       =\frac{1}{\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}}  ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle =0$,
\begin{equation*}
0=\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
   =\phi\mu S_{0}+\phi\beta\mu\,c\,(S_{0}-1)+\phi\beta+\rho S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
Removing the $O(\beta)$ self-noise term and writing
$\rho_{0}\equiv-\rho>0$ for the usual depressing offset the
zero–drift condition can be written in the familiar form

\begin{equation*}
c\,(S_{0}-1)=\frac{S_{0}}{\beta\mu}
\Bigl[\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi}-\phi\mu\Bigr]-\frac{1}{\beta\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the (dimensionless) spread

\begin{equation*}
S\equiv S_{0}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\approx 
1+\nu\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-d^{2}/2\sigma_{s}^{2}}dd ,
\end{equation*}
the mean field fixed point is $c=c^{*}$ with

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{S\kappa-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
where, up to the inessential $1/\beta$ factor which is usually absorbed
in the definition of $\phi$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= \frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}-1
          =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}-1
\end{equation*}
is the dimensionless Hebbian-depressing ratio.  In the notation in which
the filter overlap $\beta$ is absorbed in $\phi$ this is normally quoted
as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa-1=\rho/\phi\mu$, the familiar $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ Hebbian
offset.  With the explicit exponential overlap the dimensionless number
which appears in the $S$–scaled form is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=
\frac{1}{\beta}\Bigl[\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}-1\Bigr]
      =(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})\Bigl[\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}-1\Bigr]
      =\frac{\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}}{\tau_{v}}
        \Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
so that up to the conventional $\tau_{u}/\tau_{v}$ factor

$\kappa\propto1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
and with the usual sign convention $\rho<0$,
$\kappa_{+}=-\rho/\phi\mu>0$, is often written simply as
$\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ in the
convention $\rho\to-\rho$.  This is the $S\to\infty$ limit $c^{*}\to\kappa$
of the critical correlation.  The $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ form is the one
quoted in the literature; the time–constant factor $(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})/
\tau_{v}$ can be absorbed in the definition of $\phi$.  In particular
with the normalising $\tau_{v}\dot v=-v+\phi x$ used above the often
quoted dimensionless ratio is $\kappa_{*} =1+\rho/\phi\mu$, viz.
$\kappa_{*}= (\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, which for a depressing offset
$\rho<0$ is written as $\kappa_{+}=-\rho/\phi\mu$.  This is the
$\kappa$ which enters $c^{*}=(S\kappa-1)/(S-1)$. ∎

4.text:
In the mean field the two linear traces can be eliminated on the time scale
of the weight dynamics.  With $\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu $

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\,\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\,\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, with the white–noise part of the spike trains removed,

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
   =\phi w\mu^{2}S_{k}
     +\phi w\,{\cal C}\sigma^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\;,
\qquad 
{\cal C}=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}h_{u}=\frac{1}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the $w$ fixed point

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle
  = w\Big[\phi\mu^{2}S_{k}+\rho\mu S_{k}
        +\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\Big]\; .
\end{equation*}
On the translation invariant line $S_{k}\equiv S$, $w_{l}=w$, 
$\mu_{l}=\mu$ and with the usual convention $c_{kk}=0$ for the excess
correlation,
$\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=c\,(S-1)$ and $s_{kk}=1$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[S+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting $1+c\,(S-1)=\kappa S$ the mean field fixed point can be written in
the $S$–scaled form

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}\,(S-1)=\kappa S\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless Hebbian–homeostatic ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}\equiv
-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
 =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the $s$-weighted mean correlation
$\langle c\rangle_{s}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}/S_{k}=-\rho/\phi\mu$.
In the convention in which the self term $c_{kk}=1$ is kept explicitly
in the sum the $+1$ is normally absorbed in the definition of the
offset and the control parameter is quoted in the familiar $1+$ form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm eff}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
     =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
     =-\kappa_{0}+1\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the customary sign $\rho<0$ for heterosynaptic depression
$\kappa_{\rm eff}=1-|\rho|/\phi\mu>0$ and

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\,\big[1+\rho/\phi\mu\big]S-1}{S-1},
\qquad c^{*}\xrightarrow[S\to\infty]{}\!1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the $-\rho/\phi\mu$ offset, i.e. $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu>0$,
often written simply as $\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu$ or, with the $1$ kept
explicit, $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  In the un-normalised form the same
ratio is

$\displaystyle
\kappa_{0}= -\,\frac{\mu\bigl(\mu+\rho/\phi\bigr)}{{\cal C}\sigma^{2}}
     =-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\,$
up to the inessential filter overlap ${\cal C}=(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$,
which is normally absorbed in the definition of $\phi$.  With the
conventional sign $\rho\to-\theta<0$ this is the familiar positive
parameter $\kappa_{+}=|\rho|/\phi\mu$, $c^{*}\to\kappa_{+}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  This is the $-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
Hebbian drive to homeostatic offset ratio which controls the
$S$–dependence of the stability boundary.  Up to the inessential
additive unity the literature quote is $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$,
$\kappa_{d}=-\kappa_{0}>0$, i.e. the usual $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$
form. ∎

reflection_text.4:
In the mean field the two linear traces can be eliminated on the time scale
of the weight dynamics.  With $\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu $

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\,\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\,\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, with the white–noise part of the spike trains removed,

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
   =\phi w\mu^{2}S_{k}
     +\phi w\,{\cal C}\sigma^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\;,
\qquad 
{\cal C}=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}h_{u}=\frac{1}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the $w$ fixed point

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle
  = w\Big[\phi\mu^{2}S_{k}+\rho\mu S_{k}
        +\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\Big]\; .
\end{equation*}
On the translation invariant line $S_{k}\equiv S$, $w_{l}=w$, 
$\mu_{l}=\mu$ and with the usual convention $c_{kk}=0$ for the excess
correlation,
$\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=c\,(S-1)$ and $s_{kk}=1$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[S+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting $1+c\,(S-1)=\kappa S$ the mean field fixed point can be written in
the $S$–scaled form

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}\,(S-1)=\kappa S\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless Hebbian–homeostatic ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}\equiv
-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
 =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the $s$-weighted mean correlation
$\langle c\rangle_{s}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}/S_{k}=-\rho/\phi\mu$.
In the convention in which the self term $c_{kk}=1$ is kept explicitly
in the sum the $+1$ is normally absorbed in the definition of the
offset and the control parameter is quoted in the familiar $1+$ form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm eff}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
     =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
     =-\kappa_{0}+1\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the customary sign $\rho<0$ for heterosynaptic depression
$\kappa_{\rm eff}=1-|\rho|/\phi\mu>0$ and

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\,\big[1+\rho/\phi\mu\big]S-1}{S-1},
\qquad c^{*}\xrightarrow[S\to\infty]{}\!1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the $-\rho/\phi\mu$ offset, i.e. $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu>0$,
often written simply as $\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu$ or, with the $1$ kept
explicit, $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  In the un-normalised form the same
ratio is

$\displaystyle
\kappa_{0}= -\,\frac{\mu\bigl(\mu+\rho/\phi\bigr)}{{\cal C}\sigma^{2}}
     =-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\,$
up to the inessential filter overlap ${\cal C}=(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$,
which is normally absorbed in the definition of $\phi$.  With the
conventional sign $\rho\to-\theta<0$ this is the familiar positive
parameter $\kappa_{+}=|\rho|/\phi\mu$, $c^{*}\to\kappa_{+}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  This is the $-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
Hebbian drive to homeostatic offset ratio which controls the
$S$–dependence of the stability boundary.  Up to the inessential
additive unity the literature quote is $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$,
$\kappa_{d}=-\kappa_{0}>0$, i.e. the usual $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$
form. ∎

4.text:
In the mean field the two linear traces can be eliminated on the time scale
of the weight dynamics.  With $\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu $

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\,\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual white noise approximation
$\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle =\phi w\mu^{2}S_{k}+O(c)$ the mean
weight change is

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
 =\frac1{\tau_{w}}\Big[\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle\Big]
 =0\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential overall factor $w\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S_{k}-1)\Big]+\rho S_{k}=0 ,
\end{equation*}
on the translation invariant line $S_{k}\equiv S$, $w_{l}=w$,
$\mu_{l}=\mu$ and $s_{kk}=1$.  Putting

\begin{equation*}
\langle c\rangle_{s}\equiv
\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{S_{k}}
     =\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}\;,
\end{equation*}
the fixed point condition is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\,\langle c\rangle_{s}+\rho=0,
\qquad 
\langle c\rangle_{s}= -\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\equiv\kappa_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the $s$–weighted mean pairwise correlation at the fixed point is
the dimensionless Hebbian–homeostatic ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}
          =-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
\end{equation*}
in the excess-correlation convention $c_{kk}=0$.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa_{0}S ,
\qquad 
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{\,S-1\,},
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$.  With the conventional
$1+$ normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=1$,
the $+1$ is absorbed in the definition of the offset and the control
parameter is usually quoted in the $1+$ form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm eff}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
     =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
     =1+\kappa_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\bigl[\,1+\rho/\phi\mu\,\bigr]S-1}{S-1},
\qquad 
c^{*}\xrightarrow[S\to\infty]{}1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $-\rho/\phi\mu$ Hebbian drive to homeostatic offset ratio.  In the
excess–correlation convention this is the familiar $ \kappa_{0}=
-\rho/\phi\mu>0$ for the usual depressing offset $\rho<0$, often written
with the sign absorbed as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  Up to the
inessential additive unity this is the $-\rho/\phi\mu$ ratio which sets
the $S\to\infty$ limit $c^{*}\to\kappa$, i.e. $c^{*}=[\,1+\rho/\phi\mu\,]S-1
\over S-1$ in the conventional normalisation.  The filter overlap
${\cal C}=(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$ can be absorbed in $\phi$, whence the
un-normalised form $\kappa_{0}=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu\,{\cal C}
\sigma^{2}\mu^{-2}$ is obtained. ∎

3.text:
In the homogeneous mean field the two linear traces can be eliminated on the
time scale of the weight.  On the mean

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu_{k}\;, \qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the white noise normalisation
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}c_{kl}$ , $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
   =\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{k}\mu_{l}c_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
up to the inessential filter overlap which can be absorbed in $\phi$.
With $w_{l}=w$ and $\mu_{l}=\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle =0\iff 
\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle =0
\end{equation*}
is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{\sum_{l}s_{kl}}=-\frac{\rho}
{\phi\mu}\equiv\kappa_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $s$-weighted mean correlation which has to be compensated by the
homeostatic offset.  On the translationally invariant line
$S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv S$, $s_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that the zero drift condition is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=\kappa_{0}S ,\qquad 
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless homeostatic ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $s$-weighted mean correlation $\langle c\rangle_{s}$.  In the
conventional covariance normalisation
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}\!\left[1+c_{kl}\right]$ with
$c_{kk}=0$ the $1$ is kept explicitly in the correlation and the
zero drift condition is written as
$1+c(S-1)=\kappa_{*}S$ with

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{*}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}=( \kappa_{*}S-1)/(S-1)$ and
$\kappa_{*}-1=\rho/\phi\mu$.  Up to the inessential additive unity this
is $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$, $|\kappa_{0}|=|\rho|/\phi\mu>0$ for the
usual depressing offset $\rho<0$, and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$,
$c^{*}\to\kappa_{*}$ for $S\to\infty$.  The additive $+1$ is the one
conventionally absorbed in the definition of $S$ and is the form
$\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ quoted in the literature.  With the $c_{kk}=1$
convention this is simply the normalised offset $-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e.
$\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu>0$ for $\rho<0$, often quoted in the
$1+\rho/\phi\mu$ convention.  The time constants drop out of the mean
steady state $v=\phi x$, $u=\sum s w x$ and can be absorbed in the
effective variance. ∎

10.text:
In the mean field the $O(w\mu)$ part of the Hebbian drift is

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle =0\iff 
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and on the homogeneous line $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$
\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle=w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,\;\;s_{kk}=1,
\end{equation*}
so that up to the inessential filter overlap which can be absorbed in
$\phi$

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
   =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\big\langle x_{l}x_{k}\big\rangle .
\end{equation*}
With the usual $c_{kk}=1$ normalisation
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$,
$c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$, and $S_{k}=S$ on the translation invariant branch

\begin{equation*}
0=\phi\mu^{2}w\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\mu w\sum_{l}s_{kl}
   =w\mu\Big[\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1),
\qquad S=\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
so that the zero drift condition is the $s$-weighted mean correlation

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1}{S}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}
   =\langle c\rangle_{s}
   =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\qquad 
1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa_{0}S,
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless homeostatic offset

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}\equiv\langle c\rangle_{s}
       =-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\,\mu}
       =-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
for the conventional depressing sign $\rho<0$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}= \kappa_{0},
\end{equation*}
is the $s$-weighted mean correlation at marginal stability. 
With the covariance normalisation
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}\!\left[1+c_{kl}\right]$ the
$+1$ is absorbed in the definition of $S$ and the same condition is
normally quoted as

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa_{*}S,\qquad 
\kappa_{*}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa_{*}-1=\rho/\phi\mu$, so that with the depressing convention
$\rho<0$,
$\kappa_{*}=1-|\rho|/\phi\mu$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{*}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  Up to the conventional additive unity this is the
$-\rho/\phi\mu$ Hebbian-drive-to-offset ratio, often written in the
sign-free form $|\kappa|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$.  The filter overlap
${\cal C}=(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$ is inessential in the strict
steady-state $v_{k}=\phi x_{k}$, $u_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$ limit
and is normally absorbed in the effective $\phi$.  This is the
$\kappa$ which controls the $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ stability
boundary. ∎

6.text:
In the stationary mean field write the two linear traces in the form

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi \int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)x_{k}(t-s)ds,\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}(s)x_{l}(t-s)ds ,
\end{equation*}
$h_{v}(s)=\tau_{v}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v}},\;h_{u}(s)=\tau_{u}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{u}}$
and put $\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle\delta x_{k}(t)\delta x_{l}(t')\rangle
    =\sigma^{2}c_{kl}\,\delta(t-t'),\qquad c_{kk}=1 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the homogeneous ansatz $w_{l}=w,\;\mu_{l}=\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and with ${\cal C}=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}h_{u}ds=
(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle=
\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Big[\mu^{2}+\sigma^{2}{\cal C}c_{kl}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The mean weight change is

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
   =\tau_{w}^{-1}\Big[\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
     +\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle\Big]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S_{k}+\rho\mu S_{k}
+\phi\sigma^{2}{\cal C}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=0 .
\end{equation*}
On a translationally invariant branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and with
$s_{kk}=1$ 

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that up to the overall $w$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S+\rho\mu S
+\phi\sigma^{2}{\cal C}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-
S\,\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi\sigma^{2}{\cal C}}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{u}:=-
\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi\sigma^{2}{\cal C}}
=-\frac{\mu\bigl(\mu+\rho/\phi\bigr)}{\sigma^{2}{\cal C}}
\end{equation*}
the marginal stability condition can be written as

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=\kappa_{u}S ,\qquad 
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{u}S-1}{\,S-1\,}.
\end{equation*}
In the usual white–noise/Poisson normalisation
$\sigma^{2}{\cal C}/\mu^{2}\to1$, i.e. ${\cal C}\sigma^{2}\to\mu^{2}$,
and after absorbing the filter overlap ${\cal C}$ in the effective
$\phi$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\equiv\kappa_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that on the $s$–weighted mean

\begin{equation*}
\langle c\rangle_{s}\equiv\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{S}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
With the self–term kept explicit this is the familiar dimensionless
homeostatic offset

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}\,(S-1)=\kappa_{0}S,\qquad 
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa_{0}=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$.  With the conventional
covariance normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}\!\left[1+c_{kl}\right]$ the $+1$ is normally absorbed in the
definition of $S$ and the control parameter is quoted in the
$1+$ form

\begin{equation*}
\tilde\kappa\equiv 1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\qquad 
c^{*}=\frac{\tilde\kappa\,S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
so that $\tilde\kappa-1=\rho/\phi\mu$ is the Hebbian drive–to–offset
ratio.  With the opposite sign convention for the correlation,
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, this is often written as the
positive depression-to-potentiation ratio $\kappa_{+}=-\rho/\phi\mu>0$,
$\kappa_{+}= -\tilde\kappa+1$, and with the filter overlap made
explicit

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{u}=-
(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\mu\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      =-(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\Bigl[\mu+\frac{\rho}{\phi}\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
which reduces to $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\tilde\kappa=
1+\rho/\phi\mu$, on the usual unit-time normalisation
${\cal C}\sigma^{2}/\mu^{2}=1$.  The sign is convention dependent; with
the usual $\rho<0$ for net heterosynaptic LTD the magnitude is
$|\kappa_{0}|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$ and $\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa_{0}$.
This is the $-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ up to the inessential
$+1$, Hebbian drive-to-offset ratio which sets the $S\to\infty$ limit
$c^{*}\to\kappa$. ∎

8.text:
In the mean field the two traces can be eliminated on the $w$--time
scale.  With the stationary means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu_{k},\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the white noise normalisation
$\langle x_{k}(t)x_{l}(t')\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}
+\sigma^{2}c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$, $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}
   \Big[\mu^{2}+\sigma^{2}c_{kl}{\cal C}\Big] ,\qquad
{\cal C}\equiv\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)h_{u}(s)ds
        =(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that on the homogeneous line $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto
\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
 =\phi\mu^{2}wS_{k}+\rho\mu wS_{k}
   +\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}w\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},
\end{equation*}
$S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}$.  With $s_{kk}=1$, $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big) .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the translationally invariant branch $S_{k}\equiv S$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S+\rho\mu S
   +\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-
\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}}\,S
\equiv \kappa_{0}S .
\end{equation*}
Writing the fixed point condition as $1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa S$ the
$w$--nullcline is $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ with the unnormalised
mean field offset

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=-
\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{{\cal C}\sigma^{2}} 
=-\,\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless homeostatic to Hebbian ratio.  With the usual
covariance normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, $c_{kk}=1$, or equivalently
${\cal C}\sigma^{2}\to\mu^{2}$, the filter overlap is absorbed in the
definition of $\phi$ and

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\qquad 
1+c\,(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S ,
\end{equation*}
so that in the $c_{kk}=1$ convention

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\equiv\langle c\rangle_{s}
\end{equation*}
is the $s$--weighted mean correlation which has to be sustained.
With the sign convention $\rho<0$ for net LTD, $|\kappa_{0}|=
|\rho|/\phi\mu>0$ and $\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$.
If the inessential additive unity is kept explicitly in the
definition of the weighted mean,
$\langle c\rangle_{s}=[1+c(S-1)]/S$, the offset is conventionally
written in the $1$--absorbed form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{*}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\qquad 
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{*}S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa_{*}-1=\rho/\phi\mu$, the familiar
$1+\rho/\phi\mu$ Hebbian-depression ratio.  Up to the conventional
overall sign this is $\kappa\propto-\rho/\phi\mu$, often quoted as
$\kappa_{+}=-\rho/\phi\mu>0$ for $\rho<0$, the $S\to\infty$ limit
$c^{*}\to\kappa$.  The explicit filter factor ${\cal C}=
(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$ is normally absorbed in $\phi$ or in
$\sigma^{2}$; with it made explicit $\kappa_{0}=-
\mu(\phi\mu+\rho)/{\cal C}\sigma^{2}$ is the dimensionless control
parameter which sets the $c^{*}$-$S$ relation.  This is the usual
$-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ with the $+1$ absorbed, homeostatic
offset which is the $S\to\infty$ limit of the critical correlation. ∎

public_text.4:
In the mean field the two linear traces can be eliminated on the time scale
of the weight dynamics.  With $\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu $

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\,\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual white noise approximation
$\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle =\phi w\mu^{2}S_{k}+O(c)$ the mean
weight change is

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
 =\frac1{\tau_{w}}\Big[\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle\Big]
 =0\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential overall factor $w\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S_{k}-1)\Big]+\rho S_{k}=0 ,
\end{equation*}
on the translation invariant line $S_{k}\equiv S$, $w_{l}=w$,
$\mu_{l}=\mu$ and $s_{kk}=1$.  Putting

\begin{equation*}
\langle c\rangle_{s}\equiv
\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{S_{k}}
     =\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}\;,
\end{equation*}
the fixed point condition is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\,\langle c\rangle_{s}+\rho=0,
\qquad 
\langle c\rangle_{s}= -\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\equiv\kappa_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the $s$–weighted mean pairwise correlation at the fixed point is
the dimensionless Hebbian–homeostatic ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}
          =-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
\end{equation*}
in the excess-correlation convention $c_{kk}=0$.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa_{0}S ,
\qquad 
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{\,S-1\,},
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$.  With the conventional
$1+$ normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=1$,
the $+1$ is absorbed in the definition of the offset and the control
parameter is usually quoted in the $1+$ form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm eff}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
     =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
     =1+\kappa_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\bigl[\,1+\rho/\phi\mu\,\bigr]S-1}{S-1},
\qquad 
c^{*}\xrightarrow[S\to\infty]{}1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $-\rho/\phi\mu$ Hebbian drive to homeostatic offset ratio.  In the
excess–correlation convention this is the familiar $ \kappa_{0}=
-\rho/\phi\mu>0$ for the usual depressing offset $\rho<0$, often written
with the sign absorbed as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  Up to the
inessential additive unity this is the $-\rho/\phi\mu$ ratio which sets
the $S\to\infty$ limit $c^{*}\to\kappa$, i.e. $c^{*}=[\,1+\rho/\phi\mu\,]S-1
\over S-1$ in the conventional normalisation.  The filter overlap
${\cal C}=(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$ can be absorbed in $\phi$, whence the
un-normalised form $\kappa_{0}=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu\,{\cal C}
\sigma^{2}\mu^{-2}$ is obtained. ∎

public_text.3:
In the homogeneous mean field the two linear traces can be eliminated on the
time scale of the weight.  On the mean

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu_{k}\;, \qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the white noise normalisation
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}c_{kl}$ , $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
   =\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{k}\mu_{l}c_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
up to the inessential filter overlap which can be absorbed in $\phi$.
With $w_{l}=w$ and $\mu_{l}=\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle =0\iff 
\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle =0
\end{equation*}
is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{\sum_{l}s_{kl}}=-\frac{\rho}
{\phi\mu}\equiv\kappa_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $s$-weighted mean correlation which has to be compensated by the
homeostatic offset.  On the translationally invariant line
$S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv S$, $s_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that the zero drift condition is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=\kappa_{0}S ,\qquad 
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless homeostatic ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $s$-weighted mean correlation $\langle c\rangle_{s}$.  In the
conventional covariance normalisation
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}\!\left[1+c_{kl}\right]$ with
$c_{kk}=0$ the $1$ is kept explicitly in the correlation and the
zero drift condition is written as
$1+c(S-1)=\kappa_{*}S$ with

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{*}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}=( \kappa_{*}S-1)/(S-1)$ and
$\kappa_{*}-1=\rho/\phi\mu$.  Up to the inessential additive unity this
is $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$, $|\kappa_{0}|=|\rho|/\phi\mu>0$ for the
usual depressing offset $\rho<0$, and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$,
$c^{*}\to\kappa_{*}$ for $S\to\infty$.  The additive $+1$ is the one
conventionally absorbed in the definition of $S$ and is the form
$\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ quoted in the literature.  With the $c_{kk}=1$
convention this is simply the normalised offset $-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e.
$\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu>0$ for $\rho<0$, often quoted in the
$1+\rho/\phi\mu$ convention.  The time constants drop out of the mean
steady state $v=\phi x$, $u=\sum s w x$ and can be absorbed in the
effective variance. ∎

