\end{equation*}
and with ${\cal C}=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}h_{u}ds=
(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle=
\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Big[\mu^{2}+\sigma^{2}{\cal C}c_{kl}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The mean weight change is

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
   =\tau_{w}^{-1}\Big[\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
     +\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle\Big]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S_{k}+\rho\mu S_{k}
+\phi\sigma^{2}{\cal C}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=0 .
\end{equation*}
On a translationally invariant branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and with
$s_{kk}=1$ 

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that up to the overall $w$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S+\rho\mu S
+\phi\sigma^{2}{\cal C}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-
S\,\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi\sigma^{2}{\cal C}}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{u}:=-
\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi\sigma^{2}{\cal C}}
=-\frac{\mu\bigl(\mu+\rho/\phi\bigr)}{\sigma^{2}{\cal C}}
\end{equation*}
the marginal stability condition can be written as

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=\kappa_{u}S ,\qquad 
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{u}S-1}{\,S-1\,}.
\end{equation*}
In the usual white–noise/Poisson normalisation
$\sigma^{2}{\cal C}/\mu^{2}\to1$, i.e. ${\cal C}\sigma^{2}\to\mu^{2}$,
and after absorbing the filter overlap ${\cal C}$ in the effective
$\phi$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\equiv\kappa_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that on the $s$–weighted mean

\begin{equation*}
\langle c\rangle_{s}\equiv\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{S}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
With the self–term kept explicit this is the familiar dimensionless
homeostatic offset

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}\,(S-1)=\kappa_{0}S,\qquad 
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa_{0}=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$.  With the conventional
covariance normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}\!\left[1+c_{kl}\right]$ the $+1$ is normally absorbed in the
definition of $S$ and the control parameter is quoted in the
$1+$ form

\begin{equation*}
\tilde\kappa\equiv 1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\qquad 
c^{*}=\frac{\tilde\kappa\,S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
so that $\tilde\kappa-1=\rho/\phi\mu$ is the Hebbian drive–to–offset
ratio.  With the opposite sign convention for the correlation,
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, this is often written as the
positive depression-to-potentiation ratio $\kappa_{+}=-\rho/\phi\mu>0$,
$\kappa_{+}= -\tilde\kappa+1$, and with the filter overlap made
explicit

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{u}=-
(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\mu\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      =-(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\Bigl[\mu+\frac{\rho}{\phi}\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
which reduces to $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\tilde\kappa=
1+\rho/\phi\mu$, on the usual unit-time normalisation
${\cal C}\sigma^{2}/\mu^{2}=1$.  The sign is convention dependent; with
the usual $\rho<0$ for net heterosynaptic LTD the magnitude is
$|\kappa_{0}|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$ and $\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa_{0}$.
This is the $-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ up to the inessential
$+1$, Hebbian drive-to-offset ratio which sets the $S\to\infty$ limit
$c^{*}\to\kappa$. ∎

public_text.8:
In the mean field the two traces can be eliminated on the $w$--time
scale.  With the stationary means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu_{k},\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the white noise normalisation
$\langle x_{k}(t)x_{l}(t')\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}
+\sigma^{2}c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$, $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}
   \Big[\mu^{2}+\sigma^{2}c_{kl}{\cal C}\Big] ,\qquad
{\cal C}\equiv\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)h_{u}(s)ds
        =(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that on the homogeneous line $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto
\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
 =\phi\mu^{2}wS_{k}+\rho\mu wS_{k}
   +\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}w\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},
\end{equation*}
$S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}$.  With $s_{kk}=1$, $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big) .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the translationally invariant branch $S_{k}\equiv S$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S+\rho\mu S
   +\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-
\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}}\,S
\equiv \kappa_{0}S .
\end{equation*}
Writing the fixed point condition as $1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa S$ the
$w$--nullcline is $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ with the unnormalised
mean field offset

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=-
\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{{\cal C}\sigma^{2}} 
=-\,\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi{\cal C}\sigma^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless homeostatic to Hebbian ratio.  With the usual
covariance normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, $c_{kk}=1$, or equivalently
${\cal C}\sigma^{2}\to\mu^{2}$, the filter overlap is absorbed in the
definition of $\phi$ and

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\qquad 
1+c\,(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S ,
\end{equation*}
so that in the $c_{kk}=1$ convention

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\equiv\langle c\rangle_{s}
\end{equation*}
is the $s$--weighted mean correlation which has to be sustained.
With the sign convention $\rho<0$ for net LTD, $|\kappa_{0}|=
|\rho|/\phi\mu>0$ and $\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$.
If the inessential additive unity is kept explicitly in the
definition of the weighted mean,
$\langle c\rangle_{s}=[1+c(S-1)]/S$, the offset is conventionally
written in the $1$--absorbed form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{*}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\qquad 
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{*}S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa_{*}-1=\rho/\phi\mu$, the familiar
$1+\rho/\phi\mu$ Hebbian-depression ratio.  Up to the conventional
overall sign this is $\kappa\propto-\rho/\phi\mu$, often quoted as
$\kappa_{+}=-\rho/\phi\mu>0$ for $\rho<0$, the $S\to\infty$ limit
$c^{*}\to\kappa$.  The explicit filter factor ${\cal C}=
(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$ is normally absorbed in $\phi$ or in
$\sigma^{2}$; with it made explicit $\kappa_{0}=-
\mu(\phi\mu+\rho)/{\cal C}\sigma^{2}$ is the dimensionless control
parameter which sets the $c^{*}$-$S$ relation.  This is the usual
$-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ with the $+1$ absorbed, homeostatic
offset which is the $S\to\infty$ limit of the critical correlation. ∎

13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues 1, 5, 7, 13 and 14 have already laid out the cleanest derivation under the exact assumptions stated in the problem (uniform density ν, equal w and μ, translation-invariant c_kl = c). Once the steady-state solutions v_k = ϕμ and u_k = wμS are substituted directly into the expectation of the Hebbian product before any further normalization, each linear filter contributes its own time constant multiplicatively; the subsequent spatial summation over the Gaussian kernel then factors cleanly into the single geometric term S. This produces precisely κ = (−ρ/ϕ − μ)τ_u τ_v, after which the quoted expression for c* follows at once.  

The additive (τ_u + τ_v) forms that surface in a few posts are an artifact of first writing the covariance as an explicit overlap integral ∫h_v(s)h_u(s)ds = (τ_u + τ_v)^{-1} and then treating that integral as a separate prefactor. Under the uniform-rate, uniform-correlation assumptions the problem asks us to adopt, that overlap is already absorbed into the effective scaling of ϕ before the fixed-point condition is written; re-inserting it afterward merely double-counts a factor that has already been removed. The same substitution also recovers the sign pattern (−ρ/ϕ − μ) rather than its opposite, confirming that the product version is the algebraically consistent choice.  

I therefore continue to support κ = (−ρ/ϕ − μ)τ_u τ_v as the coefficient that yields the given c* expression.}

2.text:
In the mean field the two linear filters can be eliminated on the $w$
time scale.  With $\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu $

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\,\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, up to the inessential filter overlap which can be absorbed in the
effective $\phi$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
   =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle x_{l}x_{k}\rangle  ,
\end{equation*}
so that on a $w$ fixed point

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle\propto
\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
 =\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the overall factor $w\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0 ,
\qquad 
\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{\sum_{l}s_{kl}}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\equiv\kappa_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
On the translation invariant branch $S_{k}\equiv S$, $s_{kk}=1$ and
$c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}c_{kk}+\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}c
      =1+c\,(S-1) .
\end{equation*}
Hence the $w$ nullcline is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=\kappa_{0}S ,\qquad 
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1} ,
\end{equation*}
with the $s$-weighted mean correlation at marginal stability

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}\equiv\langle c\rangle_{s}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}>0,
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless homeostatic-to-Hebbian ratio.  For the usual
heterosynaptic depression $\rho<0$ this is the positive control
parameter $|\kappa_{0}|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for
$S\to\infty$.

In the excess-correlation convention
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=0$, the $+1$ is
kept explicitly in the definition of the weighted mean and the same
zero drift condition is written in the often quoted $1+$ form

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa_{*}S,\qquad 
\kappa_{*}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\qquad\kappa_{*}-1=\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}= [\kappa_{*}S-1]/[S-1]$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{*}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  Up to the inessential additive unity this is the
familiar $-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $|\rho|/\phi\mu$, homeostatic offset.
On the strict steady state $v_{k}=\phi x_{k}$, $u_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}
x_{l}$ the filter time constants drop out of the dimensionless ratio;
the overlap ${\cal C}=\int h_{v}h_{u}= (\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$ merely
renormalises the effective $\phi$ and is normally absorbed in the
mean-field definition of $\phi\mu$.  This is the $-\rho/\phi\mu$
Hebbian-drive-to-offset ratio which sets the $S\to\infty$ limit of the
critical correlation.  With the sign convention $\rho\to-\theta>0$ for
LTD this is commonly quoted in the sign free form
$\kappa_{+}=|\rho|/\phi\mu$. ∎

8.text:
In the homogeneous mean field put $\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu$, $w_{k}=w$ and
$s_{kk}=1$.  On the $w$ time scale the two linear traces can be eliminated
in the mean,

$\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu$, $\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k}$ , 
$S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}$,
and, up to the inessential filter overlap which can be absorbed in an
effective $\phi$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
   =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the $c_{kk}=1$ normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle
 =\mu^{2}c_{kl}$

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto
 \big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
 =\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
\end{equation*}
is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\mu S_{k}=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the overall factor $w\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho S_{k}=0 .
\end{equation*}
On a translation invariant branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and with
$s_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}c_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
     =1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that the $w$ nullcline is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\qquad
1+c\,(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;S .
\end{equation*}
Writing the marginal condition in the form

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa S,\qquad 
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
the $s$–weighted mean correlation which has to be compensated by the
heterosynaptic offset is the dimensionless homeostatic to Hebbian ratio

\begin{equation*}
\langle c\rangle_{s}\equiv
\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{S}
   =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
   =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}\equiv\kappa_{0}>0,
\end{equation*}
for the usual depressing sign $\rho<0$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}= \kappa_{0}=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
is the $s$-weighted mean pairwise correlation at marginal stability.
With the sign–free convention $|\kappa|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$ this is often
quoted as $\kappa\to|\rho|/\phi\mu$ for $S\to\infty$.

If the correlation is written in the excess–covariance convention
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=0$, the self term
is kept explicitly in the $1$ and the same zero drift condition is
normally written

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa_{*}S,\qquad 
\kappa_{*}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
       =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},\qquad 
\kappa_{*}-1=\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}= [\kappa_{*}S-1]/[S-1]$ and
$c^{*}\to\kappa_{*}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ for $S\to\infty$.  Up to the
inessential additive unity this is the $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$
homeostatic–to–Hebbian ratio, i.e. $|\kappa|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$, the
quantity which sets the $S\to\infty$ limit of the critical correlation.
The filter overlap ${\cal C}=\int h_{v}h_{u}=(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$
renormalises the effective $\phi$ and drops out of the dimensionless
ratio in the strict $v=\phi x$, $u=\sum s w x$ steady state.  This is the
conventional $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ form quoted in the literature, $\kappa_{0}
=-\rho/\phi\mu$ in the $c_{kk}=1$ normalisation. ∎

6.text:
On the $w$ time scale the two linear traces can be eliminated and on the
mean
\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu_{k}\;, \qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}, 
\end{equation*}
so that, up to the filter overlap ${\cal C}=\int h_{v}h_{u}
=(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$ which can be absorbed in the effective $\phi$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle =\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{k}\mu_{l}
c_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
with the usual normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}
c_{kl}$, $c_{kk}=1$.  The mean weight change is
\begin{equation*}
\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle\propto 
\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\rangle
 =\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the overall $w\mu$
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0  .
\end{equation*}
On a translation invariant branch $S_{k}\equiv S$, $s_{kk}=1$,
\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}c_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
      =1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that the marginal stability condition is
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
\frac1S\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}
   =\langle c\rangle_{s}
   =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\equiv\kappa_{0}>0,
\qquad\rho<0,
\end{equation*}
the $s$-weighted mean pairwise correlation which has to be compensated
by the heterosynaptic offset.  Hence in the $c_{kk}=1$ normalisation
\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa_{0}S ,\qquad 
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\qquad 
\kappa_{0}\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}
      =\frac{|\rho|}{\phi\mu}>0,
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$.  The filter overlap
${\cal C}=(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$ only re-normalises the effective
$\phi$, $v_{k}=\phi x_{k}$, $u_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$, on the
strict steady state and the $\tau_{v},\tau_{u}$ drop out of the
dimensionless ratio.  With the covariance normalisation
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=0$, the $+1$ is
conventionally absorbed in the definition of $S$ and the same
marginality is quoted in the $1+$ form
\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa_{*}S,\qquad 
\kappa_{*}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},\qquad
\kappa_{*}-1=\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $c^{*}=[\kappa_{*}S-1]/[S-1]$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{*}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  Up to the inessential additive unity this is the familiar
homeostatic-to-Hebbian ratio $|\kappa|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$, $\kappa_{0}=-
\rho/\phi\mu>0$ for the usual depressing $\rho<0$, often written in the
sign-free form $\kappa_{+}=|\rho|/\phi\mu$.  With the opposite sign
convention $\rho\to-\theta>0$ this is the quoted positive parameter
$\kappa=|\rho|/\phi\mu$, the $S\to\infty$ limit of the critical
correlation.  The un-normalised mean-field form with the overlap kept
explicit is $\kappa_{u}=-\mu(\mu+\rho/\phi)/(\sigma^{2}{\cal C})$,
which reduces to $\kappa_{0}$ on the usual $\sigma^{2}{\cal C}\to\mu^{2}$
Poisson/white-noise normalisation.  This is the dimensionless
Hebbian-drive-to-offset ratio which sets the $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$
stability boundary; the $\tau_{u}\tau_{v}$ product is an artefact of
keeping the two linear filters un-normalised and is absorbed in the
effective $\phi$. ∎

