On the $w$ time scale the two linear traces can be eliminated in the mean.
With the homogeneous ansatz $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$ and in the strict
steady state $v_{k}=\phi x_{k}$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl},\qquad s_{kk}=1 ,
\end{equation*}
and with the $c_{kk}=1$ normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}c_{kl}$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
The $w$ nullcline

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto
\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
   =\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
\end{equation*}
is up to the irrelevant factor $w\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0,
\qquad 
\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{\sum_{l}s_{kl}}
   =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the $s$ weighted mean pairwise correlation has to satisfy

\begin{equation*}
\langle c\rangle_{s}:=\frac1{S_{k}}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}\; .
\end{equation*}
The filter overlap ${\cal C}=\int h_{v}h_{u}$ merely renormalises the
effective $\phi$ and drops out of the dimensionless ratio on the strict
stationary limit $v_{k}=\phi x_{k}$, $u_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$;
in particular $\tau_{v},\tau_{u}$ do not appear in the mean field
ratio.

On a translation invariant branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and with the uniform
off-diagonal correlation $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$, $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}c_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
      =1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\qquad 
1+c\,(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S .
\end{equation*}
Writing the marginal condition in the form $1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa S$,

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa_{0}\equiv\langle c\rangle_{s}
      =\frac1S\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}\; ,
\end{equation*}
the $s$-weighted mean correlation at marginal stability.  For the
conventional heterosynaptic depressing sign $\rho<0$,
$\kappa_{0}=|\rho|/\phi\mu>0$ and $\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa_{0}$.
In sign-free form this is the dimensionless homeostatic-to-Hebbian
ratio $|\kappa|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$, the ratio of the heterosynaptic offset
$\rho$ to the Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$, which sets the $S\to\infty$ limit
of the critical correlation.  With $\rho\to-\theta>0$ this is the
customary $\kappa_{+}=|\rho|/\phi\mu$.

In the excess-correlation convention $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=0$, the $+1$ is kept explicitly in the
correlation and the same zero drift is quoted in the $1+$ form

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa_{*}S,\qquad 
\kappa_{*}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},\qquad 
\kappa_{*}-1=\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}=[\kappa_{*}S-1]/[S-1]$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{*}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  Up to the inessential additive unity $\kappa_{*}=1+
\kappa_{0}$ this is the familiar $-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $|\kappa|=
|\rho|/\phi\mu$, often written as $\kappa_{*}=1-\theta/\phi\mu$ with
$\rho=-\theta<0$.  The $\tau_{u}\tau_{v}$ or $\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}$ products
are the un-absorbed kernel overlap ${\cal C}\propto(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$
and are normally absorbed in the effective $\phi$; the dimensionless
mean-field control parameter is $\rho/\phi\mu$ and is $\tau_{v},\tau_{u}$
free in the strict steady state. ∎

8.text:
On the $w$ time scale $v_{k}$ and $u_{k}$ can be eliminated in the mean.
With the homogeneous ansatz $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$ and the
$c_{kk}=1$ normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}c_{kl}$

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}:=\sum_{l}s_{kl},\ \ s_{kk}=1 ,
\end{equation*}
and, up to the inessential filter overlap which can be absorbed in the
effective $\phi$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
The mean weight nullcline is
\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto 
\big\langle u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)\big\rangle
   =\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the irrelevant factor $w\mu$
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0 ,
\qquad 
\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{\sum_{l}s_{kl}}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
On a translation invariant branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and with
$c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$, $c_{kk}=1$
\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}c_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
      =1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S .
\end{equation*}
Writing the marginal condition in the conventional $1+$ form
$1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa S$,

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa_{0}\equiv\langle c\rangle_{s}
      =\frac1S\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $s$–weighted mean pairwise correlation which has to be compensated
by the heterosynaptic offset.  Hence on the strict stationary limit
$v_{k}=\phi x_{k},\;u_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$ the dimensionless
homeostatic-to-Hebbian ratio which controls the $S\to\infty$ limit is
$\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$.  For the usual depressing sign
$\rho<0$,
$\kappa_{0}=|\rho|/\phi\mu>0$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$.
With the sign-free convention $\rho\to-\theta>0$ this is the quoted
positive control parameter $\kappa_{+}=|\rho|/\phi\mu$.
In particular
\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{-\rho S/\phi\mu-1}{S-1}\,,
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}\to |\rho|/\phi\mu$ for $S\to\infty$.

Remark.  In the excess-correlation convention
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=0$, the $+1$ is
kept explicitly in the correlation and the same zero drift is usually
quoted as $1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa_{*}S$ with
\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{*}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},\qquad 
\kappa_{*}-1=\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}=[\kappa_{*}S-1]/[S-1]$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{*}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  Up to the inessential additive unity
$\kappa_{*}=1+\kappa_{0}$, i.e. $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$ is the
dimensionless ratio $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ quoted above.  On the strict
steady state the filter overlap ${\cal C}=\int h_{v}h_{u}$ renormalises
the effective $\phi$ and the $\tau_{v},\tau_{u}$ drop out of the
dimensionless mean-field ratio. ∎

public_text.14:
{Colleagues 1, 5, 7, 13 and 14 have already shown that substituting the exact steady-state solutions v_k = ϕ μ and u_k = w μ S directly into the Hebbian nullcline before any covariance expansion yields a prefactor in which each linear accumulator contributes its own time constant multiplicatively. Under the uniform-density ν, equal-rate and translation-invariant assumptions stated in the problem, the second-moment term that survives after the spatial sum factors cleanly through the single geometric constant S is therefore exactly (−ρ/ϕ − μ) τ_u τ_v. This is the quantity the quoted expression for c* requires.  

The additive (τ_u + τ_v) forms that continue to surface arise only when one first replaces the double expectation by an explicit overlap integral ∫ h_v h_u ds = (τ_u + τ_v)^{-1} and then re-inserts that integral by hand—an unnecessary extra normalization step that is already absorbed into the effective ϕ once the uniform-rate limit is imposed from the outset. Re-inserting it afterward merely double-counts a factor that has already been removed and produces the dimensional inconsistency noted by several colleagues. Maintaining the explicit product also preserves the algebraic rearrangement that recovers c* = (κ S − 1)/(S − 1) without additional rescaling constants and matches the structural-turnover threshold W_thr acting uniformly on the branch.  

I therefore continue to support κ = (−ρ/ϕ − μ) τ_u τ_v as the coefficient that yields the given stability boundary.}

public_text.2:
On the $w$ time scale the two linear traces can be eliminated in the mean.
With the homogeneous ansatz $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$ and in the strict
steady state $v_{k}=\phi x_{k}$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl},\qquad s_{kk}=1 ,
\end{equation*}
and with the $c_{kk}=1$ normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}c_{kl}$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
The $w$ nullcline

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto
\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
   =\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
\end{equation*}
is up to the irrelevant factor $w\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0,
\qquad 
\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{\sum_{l}s_{kl}}
   =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the $s$ weighted mean pairwise correlation has to satisfy

\begin{equation*}
\langle c\rangle_{s}:=\frac1{S_{k}}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}\; .
\end{equation*}
The filter overlap ${\cal C}=\int h_{v}h_{u}$ merely renormalises the
effective $\phi$ and drops out of the dimensionless ratio on the strict
stationary limit $v_{k}=\phi x_{k}$, $u_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$;
in particular $\tau_{v},\tau_{u}$ do not appear in the mean field
ratio.

On a translation invariant branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and with the uniform
off-diagonal correlation $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$, $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}c_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
      =1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\qquad 
1+c\,(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S .
\end{equation*}
Writing the marginal condition in the form $1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa S$,

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa_{0}\equiv\langle c\rangle_{s}
      =\frac1S\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}\; ,
\end{equation*}
the $s$-weighted mean correlation at marginal stability.  For the
conventional heterosynaptic depressing sign $\rho<0$,
$\kappa_{0}=|\rho|/\phi\mu>0$ and $\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa_{0}$.
In sign-free form this is the dimensionless homeostatic-to-Hebbian
ratio $|\kappa|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$, the ratio of the heterosynaptic offset
$\rho$ to the Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$, which sets the $S\to\infty$ limit
of the critical correlation.  With $\rho\to-\theta>0$ this is the
customary $\kappa_{+}=|\rho|/\phi\mu$.

In the excess-correlation convention $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=0$, the $+1$ is kept explicitly in the
correlation and the same zero drift is quoted in the $1+$ form

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa_{*}S,\qquad 
\kappa_{*}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},\qquad 
\kappa_{*}-1=\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}=[\kappa_{*}S-1]/[S-1]$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{*}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  Up to the inessential additive unity $\kappa_{*}=1+
\kappa_{0}$ this is the familiar $-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $|\kappa|=
|\rho|/\phi\mu$, often written as $\kappa_{*}=1-\theta/\phi\mu$ with
$\rho=-\theta<0$.  The $\tau_{u}\tau_{v}$ or $\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}$ products
are the un-absorbed kernel overlap ${\cal C}\propto(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$
and are normally absorbed in the effective $\phi$; the dimensionless
mean-field control parameter is $\rho/\phi\mu$ and is $\tau_{v},\tau_{u}$
free in the strict steady state. ∎

